Complete Javascript methods reference
A. Mutator Methods (Modify the original array)

1. Array.prototype.push()
Signature: arr.push(element1[, ...[, elementN]])
Returns: The new length of the array
Description: Adds one or more elements to the end of an array.

const fruits = ['apple', 'banana'];
const newLength = fruits.push('orange', 'mango');
// fruits: ['apple', 'banana', 'orange', 'mango']
// newLength: 4

2. Array.prototype.pop()
Signature: arr.pop()
Returns: The removed element or undefined if empty
Description: Removes the last element from an array.

const fruits = ['apple', 'banana', 'orange'];
const last = fruits.pop();
// fruits: ['apple', 'banana']
// last: 'orange'

3. Array.prototype.shift()
Signature: arr.shift()
Returns: The removed element or undefined if empty
Description: Removes the first element from an array.

const fruits = ['apple', 'banana', 'orange'];
const first = fruits.shift();
// fruits: ['banana', 'orange']
// first: 'apple'

4. Array.prototype.unshift()
Signature: arr.unshift(element1[, ...[, elementN]])
Returns: The new length of the array
Description: Adds one or more elements to the beginning of an array.

const fruits = ['banana', 'orange'];
const newLength = fruits.unshift('apple', 'mango');
// fruits: ['apple', 'mango', 'banana', 'orange']
// newLength: 4

5. Array.prototype.splice()
Signature: arr.splice(start[, deleteCount[, item1[, ...[, itemN]]])
Returns: An array containing the deleted elements
Description: Changes the contents of an array by removing or replacing existing elements and/or adding new elements.

const months = ['Jan', 'March', 'April', 'June'];
// Insert at index 1, delete 0 elements
months.splice(1, 0, 'Feb');
// months: ['Jan', 'Feb', 'March', 'April', 'June']

// Replace 1 element at index 4
const deleted = months.splice(4, 1, 'May');
// months: ['Jan', 'Feb', 'March', 'April', 'May']
// deleted: ['June']

6. Array.prototype.sort()
Signature: arr.sort([compareFunction])
Returns: The sorted array (original array is sorted)
Description: Sorts the elements of an array in place.

By default, sort method sorts array elements as strings in lexicographical order. If you want to sort numbers
you should pass a comparison function to ensure the correct numerical order
Lexicographical order refers to the way of sorting words or strings based on their alphabetical sequence,
similar to how words are listed in a dictionary.
It compares the strings character by character, from left to right.

Key Points:
Character-by-character comparison: Lexicographical order compares the characters in the strings one by one,
using their Unicode (or ASCII) values.

Order of letters: It works similarly to alphabetical order, where "a" comes before "b", "b" comes before "c", and so on.
However, it’s important to note that lexicographical order is case-sensitive, meaning uppercase letters come before
lowercase letters because of their lower ASCII/Unicode values.

let numbers = [10, 5, 8, 2, 7];
numbers.sort((a, b) => a - b);  // Ascending order
console.log(numbers);  // Output: [2, 5, 7, 8, 10]

let numbers = [10, 5, 8, 2, 7];
numbers.sort((a, b) => b - a);  // Descending order
console.log(numbers);  // Output: [10, 8, 7, 5, 2]

For String Arrays: If you are sorting an array of strings, sort() will handle the lexicographical order by default
let fruits = ['banana', 'apple', 'cherry', 'mango'];
fruits.sort();  // Ascending lexicographical order
console.log(fruits);  // Output: ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry', 'mango']

let fruits = ['banana', 'apple', 'cherry', 'mango'];
fruits.sort().reverse();  // Ascending first then reverse for descending order
console.log(fruits);  // Output: ['mango', 'cherry', 'banana', 'apple']
